data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work as expected:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signedSpan   = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> unsignedSpan = stackalloc nuint[5];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes. The values are read/written through the span just like any other unmanaged type. The only caveat is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context since the size isn't fixed at compile time — use `IntPtr.Size` in safe code instead.
